.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt DTFL_EVENT_WRITER_NEW 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtfl_event_writer_new ,
.Nm dtfl_event_writer_free ,
.Nm dtfl_event_writer_pusher ,
.Nm dtfl_event_reader_replay
.Nd capture a stream to a file descriptor and replay it back
.Sh LIBRARY
Timely and differential dataflow library
.Pq libdtfl , \-ldtfl
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In dtfl/timely_ops.h
.Ft dtfl_event_writer *
.Fn dtfl_event_writer_new "int fd" "const dtfl_time_class *tc" "const dtfl_type *type"
.Ft void
.Fn dtfl_event_writer_free "dtfl_event_writer *w"
.Ft dtfl_event_pusher
.Fn dtfl_event_writer_pusher "dtfl_event_writer *w"
.Ft dtfl_stream *
.Fn dtfl_event_reader_replay "int const *fds" "size_t nfds" "dtfl_scope *scope" "const dtfl_type *type"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions carry a captured stream over a byte stream, so a stream
captured in one process can be replayed in another, the binary form of
the in-memory event link
.Pq Xr dtfl_event_link_replay 3 .
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_event_writer_new
wraps a writable file descriptor
.Fa fd
in a writer for a stream of time class
.Fa tc
and record type
.Fa type .
The writer does not own
.Fa fd .
.Fn dtfl_event_writer_pusher
returns an event pusher suitable for
.Xr dtfl_stream_capture_into 3 :
each captured event is written to the descriptor as a frame, a little
endian
.Vt u64
length followed by the encoded event.
.Fn dtfl_event_writer_free
releases the writer; it does not close the descriptor.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_event_reader_replay
is the reading counterpart.
It reads the frames written by one or more writers from the
.Fa nfds
descriptors in
.Fa fds
and reconstructs the captured stream in
.Fa scope ,
holding one initial capability per descriptor as the replay protocol
requires.
The descriptors may be non-blocking; the operator re-activates itself so
a stream still being written keeps draining.
The record type must match the type the writers were created with.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn dtfl_event_writer_new
returns a new writer.
.Fn dtfl_event_writer_pusher
returns a pusher borrowing the writer.
.Fn dtfl_event_reader_replay
returns the replayed stream, owned by
.Fa scope .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/* sender */
dtfl_event_writer *w = dtfl_event_writer_new(sock, tc, dtfl_type_u64());
dtfl_stream_capture_into(s, dtfl_event_writer_pusher(w), DTFL_NOCTX);

/* receiver */
int fds[1] = { sock };
dtfl_stream *s = dtfl_event_reader_replay(fds, 1, scope, dtfl_type_u64());
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
These functions do not return error codes.
A write or read failure on the descriptor calls the panic hook, as the
pusher interface has no way to report an error.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtfl_event_link_replay 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_stream_capture 3 ,
.Xr dtfl 7
.Sh HISTORY
This is the C form of timely's
.Vt EventWriter
and
.Vt EventReader
of
.Pa capture/event.rs
and the
.Fn replay_into
operator of
.Pa capture/replay.rs .
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
A writer and the stream and operator built by
.Fn dtfl_event_reader_replay
belong to the worker thread that created them.
The underlying descriptors may connect threads or processes; the byte
framing carries the ordering the replay protocol needs.
